/usr/bin/detab from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for detab, so this is the disassembly, and detab.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f detab.ctl detab.dist

detab.dist is the binary off inode 57 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 3407
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It is the second distribution disk,
and /usr/bin, not /bin.

It has no header.  The first word is code and not the object magic, so the
kernel's exec loads the whole file as text at 0100 - see ../newuser/README
for the same thing - and the text runs to 0e4f, with its data after it.  The
runtime is Whitesmith's, the same as newuser's: c.ent at 0d9f, c.ents at
0c52.

What it is: main at 010e, which calls getc on the standard input until the
end of the file and writes each character with putc to the standard output.
A tab writes a blank and then blanks until the column is one more than a
multiple of eight (0192 is the test); a newline writes itself and starts the
column at 1; anything else writes itself and moves the column on by one.  The
column is a word at 0dd1 that starts at 1.  Nothing else is looked at, so a
backspace or a carriage return is a column like any other.  The 1.6 manual
page says as much and no more: tabs at 9, 17, 25.  The rest is the library,
its stdio from 01cb and its exit at 09a9.

detab.c is that, written with getchar and putchar.  It is byte for byte the
same output on every input tried.

The exit status is the one difference.  main falls out through c.rets with
the last getc's -1 still in BC, the startup passes that to exit, and so every
run of the original ends with status 255.  This exits 0.

Checked in usersim against the original, on a small text with tabs, three
thousand random characters of blanks, tabs, newlines, carriage returns and
backspaces, and an empty file: the same bytes each time.

It is installed in /usr/bin, where the distribution had it - see the
GNUmakefile - and it is not installed over the sim's copy until someone does
that.
